push("ei46q3", r`Source: Ryan T. Godwin, Econometrics I, section 4.6, exercise 3, p. 43. CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. https://rtgodwin.com/econometrics1.pdf`, r`3. The M matrix is sometimes referred to as a “residual-maker” matrix. That  is, MQ q produces the LS residuals from a regression of the vector q on the matrix Q. Let M1 = I − X1 (X1′ X1 )−1 X1′ . Prove that M1 y is equal to the LS residuals of a regression of y on X1 .`, r`Answer. But (X1′ X1 )−1 X1′ y is just the LS estimator from a regression of y on X1 , so: where e1 are the residuals from an LS regression of y on X1 .`);

push("ei46q4", r`Source: Ryan T. Godwin, Econometrics I, section 4.6, exercise 4, p. 43. CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. https://rtgodwin.com/econometrics1.pdf`, r`4. Prove that if the data is transformed using M1 , then X1 may be dropped from the regression model.`, r`Answer. This is the same as the FWL proof in Section 4.3.5.`);

push("ei54q123", r`Source: Ryan T. Godwin, Econometrics I, section 5.4, exercises 1–3, p. 52. CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. https://rtgodwin.com/econometrics1.pdf`, r`1. Explain why the LS estimator is a random variable.`, r`Answer. b is a linear function of y, and y is a linear function of ϵ. Since ϵ is random, so is b. 2. Show that the LS estimator is linear. Answer. See Section 5.2. 3. Prove that the LS estimator is unbiased. Answer. See Section 5.1.`);

push("ei54q4", r`Source: Ryan T. Godwin, Econometrics I, section 5.4, exercise 4, p. 52. CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. https://rtgodwin.com/econometrics1.pdf`, r`4. Derive the variance of the LS estimator, stating any assumptions that you use.`, r`Answer. See the derivation of equation 5.3 in Section 5.3.`);

push("ei54q5", r`Source: Ryan T. Godwin, Econometrics I, section 5.4, exercise 5, p. 52–53. CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. https://rtgodwin.com/econometrics1.pdf`, r`5. Explain the result of the Gauss-Markov theorem, and why it is important.`, r`Answer. The Gauss-Markov theorem states that V(β̂) − V(b) is a positive-semi-definite matrix, where β̂ is any linear and unbiased estimator for β and b is the LS estimator. That is, the LS estimator has the lowest variance out of the entire class of linear and unbiased estimators for β. This is important because there are many such possible estimators. The G-M theorem explains a reason why LS might be the “best” choice among them.`);

push("ei54q6", r`Source: Ryan T. Godwin, Econometrics I, section 5.4, exercise 6, p. 53. CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. https://rtgodwin.com/econometrics1.pdf`, r`6. Suppose that we have our usual linear model: 5.4 Exercises but that we partition the X matrix and β vector and write the model as: All of the usual assumptions are satisfied, except that E[ϵ] = X1 γ. That is, the mean vector for the disturbances is a linear combination of a subset of the regressors (implying that ϵ and X1 are correlated). Let b1 and b2 be the OLS estimators for β 1 and β 2 . Obtain the expressions for E[b1 ] and E[b2 ], and interpret your results.`, r`Answer. The LS estimator for β 1 is: where M2 = I − X2 (X2′ X2 )−1 X2′ . Substituting in the population model for y, and expanding, we have: Using M2 X2 = 0, and simplifying, we get: Finally, taking the expectation of b1 and using E [ϵ] = X1 γ we get: We will get a similar result for E [b2 ], except that the third term will cancel since M1 X1 = 0: A critical assumption for the unbiasedness of the OLS estimator is that the regressors are unrelated to the error term (assumption A.5). The results above show that if some of the regressors (X1 for example) are related to the error term, then only those corresponding LS estimators will be biased (b1 ). b2 remains unbiased as long as X2 is unrelated to ϵ.`);

