push("ei116q8", r`Source: Ryan T. Godwin, Econometrics I, section 11.6, exercises 8–11, p. 111. CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. https://rtgodwin.com/econometrics1.pdf`, r`8. Derive the GLS estimator, by transforming the error term so that it is homoskedastic. 9. Show that LS is inefficient in the presence of heteroskedasticity, by arguing that GLS is efficient. 10. Explain how we only need to know the relative differences in variance between observations (the proportionality of the variances), in order to perform GLS. 11. Show how to implement GLS when the data is “clustered”.`);

